course: dictionary
lesson: 1347
title: Can: ability, permission and cans
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Can is a small word with many jobs, so context tells you which one it has. Most often it means is able to: «I can swim» means you have the ability to swim. Can also means is allowed to: «You can go out now» gives you permission.
explain: Can asks politely too: «Can I have some water?» Can also shows what is possible: «Animals can get trapped in plastic rings by the neck, beak or legs.» The sentence does not say they always do, only that it is possible.
explain: Can is also a noun. «A can of beans» means a metal container for food or drink. To check which meaning you have, swap in another word, such as is able to, is allowed to, or may. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Can is a modal verb, so it is followed by another verb without «to»: «I can swim», not «I can to swim».
fact: Can means is able to, is allowed to, or is possible.
fact: «A can of beans» uses can as a noun: a metal container for food or drink.
fact: In formal writing, can is often replaced by «may», «is permitted to» or «is able to».
fact: «Can't» is the short form of cannot, and it means is not able to or is not allowed to.
exercise: In «I can swim», what does can mean? | *Is able to | Is finished | Is a metal container | Is allowed to pay
exercise: In «You can go out now», what does can mean? | Is finished | A metal container for food | Is able to swim | *Is allowed to
exercise: Which word in «Animals can get trapped in plastic rings by the neck» shows that something is possible? | *can | neck | rings | animals
test: In «a can of beans», what is a can? | A short form of cannot | *A metal container for food or drink | A kind of permission | Something you are able to do
test: Which sentence uses can to ask politely? | You can go out now | *Can I have some water? | It can get cold at night | I can swim
exam: In formal writing, which word could replace can in «You can go out now»? | cannot | canned | cans | *may
